iently so this comes in the form of  tools from continuous optimization and  uh more classical algorithm design such  as numerical linear algebra data  structures things like this  um on the other hand uh in order to  obtain true uh like truly optimal  methods statistically it often uh you  have to often understand the structure  of the data so uh namely this is high  dimensional statistics where you draw  upon things like uh geometric uh  properties of the data concentration  phenomena and so on  um  and in fact uh many problems in modern  data science benefit from understanding  the toolkits of each of these particular  fields but to obtain sort of the best of  all worlds often you have to uh have a  careful interplay between  the uh the techniques of both  so um i guess these are sort of like a  broad overview of the topics that i'm  i'm interested in have worked on to some  extent so if you if you like any of  these i'm sure we have things to chat  about but uh for this talk i'd like to  focus on two in particular um which  really capture the interplay between  these two uh sets of techniques  okay so the first half will be about  logging of sampling and the second half  will be about a robust estimation  okay so uh diving right into it uh we're  going to consider the problem of  composite log to give sampling where uh  just as an example you might try to draw  a  a sample from a multivariate normal  distribution i'll subject to box  constraints on the uh on the parameters  so namely you can think of these as like  parameter ranges for each of your each  of your dimensions  so um  introducing a little bit more more  notation  um this is the problem of drawing a  sample from a density proportional to  minus f minus g  where f here captures the uh the  multivariate normal part and g is the  restriction to a box namely it's zero  inside the box and infinity uh  everywhere else okay  so um a generalization of this problem  is uh the following setup where f is  some 